# reading 2 of hume-induction.md by claude-opus-5-5
# Hume's case that no argument, demonstrative or probable, engages us to trust past experience.
# S3 and S4 are rhetorical questions offered as cases of what can be conceived; S3 is taken as asserting conceivability.
# The "can" and "must" in S2, S5 and S6 are read as non-modal (plain implication), so no modal logic is used.
atom	justified	we are engaged by arguments to put trust in past experience and make it the standard of our future judgement
atom	demo	there is a demonstrative argument (concerning relations of ideas) that the future will be conformable to the past
atom	probable	there is a probable argument (concerning matter of fact and existence) that the future will be conformable to the past
atom	contra	it implies a contradiction that the course of nature may change
atom	conceivable	that the course of nature may change, and like objects be attended with contrary effects, can be clearly and distinctly conceived
atom	causal	all arguments concerning existence are founded on the relation of cause and effect
atom	fromexp	our knowledge of the relation of cause and effect is derived entirely from experience
atom	presupp	all our experimental conclusions proceed upon the supposition that the future will be conformable to the past
atom	circular	proving the supposition that the future will be conformable to the past by probable arguments is going in a circle, taking for granted the point in question
P1	S1	justified → demo ∨ probable	kind conceptual
P2	S3	conceivable
P3	S5	conceivable → ¬contra	kind conceptual
C1	S2	¬contra	from P2 P3
P4	S5	¬contra → ¬demo	kind conceptual
C2	S2	¬demo	from C1 P4
C3	S6	justified → probable	from P1 C2
P5	S8	causal
P6	S8	fromexp
P7	S8	presupp
# The "therefore" in S9 draws circularity from the three S8 claims; the link is unstated.
A1	-	causal ∧ fromexp ∧ presupp → circular
C4	S9	circular	from P5 P6 P7 A1
# Unstated: a circular proof is no argument of this kind.
A2	-	circular → ¬probable
C5	S7	¬probable	from C4 A2
# S7's "no argument of this kind", together with S6, yields that we are not engaged by arguments.
C	S7	¬justified	from C3 C5
